[
    {
        "content": "<p>Here's <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/pull/309\">my first PR</a>, addressing the good-first-issue <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/issues/103\">#103</a> on GitHub. </p>\n<p>I was wondering a couple of things: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Is there some kind of linter which I can use to format my files? I have tried to keep the spacing conventions of mathlib, but I can't promise that all of them are followed. I'd also prefer an automated solution over me going over the whole file manually</li>\n<li>How does the authors section work? Is it a \"mark yourself in the files you think you have made the most interesting contributions\", or strictly \"all files you have touched in this PR\"? I've gone with the first approach for now since it seems the most sensible.</li>\n</ol>",
        "id": 584680299,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1775829087
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Nice! I'll do my best to look over this soon. </p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>I find though that if you have access to any AI agents you can point them to the mathlib style and naming guides, and they do a relatively good job at cleaning up the minor errors. Otherwise, I am the linter. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>We're thinking of changing this. As a rule of thumb, if you think you've made enough of a change that you would be the right person to explain it, you should be on the authors list.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>",
        "id": 584685353,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1775830366
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I've added a couple of the missing lemmas, mainly those to do with <code>bigSepL2</code> and <code>bigSepM</code>. What's left are those definitions which make use of <code>bigOpM2</code> and <code>bigOpS</code>, which have not been implemented yet, I believe.</p>",
        "id": 585164398,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1776079734
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>By the way, I wanted to ask about the <code>PartialMap</code> API, and what are our plans on it. I found it a bit painful to use since many times the type arguments such as <code>M</code> and <code>K</code> need to be given explicitly, although I'm not sure how we could fix it.</p>",
        "id": 585164999,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1776079903
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'll see what I can do when I finish refactoring your PR, and then we can revisit it!</p>",
        "id": 585165381,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1776080010
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"690100\">Fernando LEAL</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/490604-iris-lean/topic/Adding.20missing.20definitions.20to.20.60Plainly.2Elean.60/near/585164398\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I've added a couple of the missing lemmas, mainly those to do with <code>bigSepL2</code> and <code>bigSepM</code>. What's left are those definitions which make use of <code>bigOpM2</code> and <code>bigOpS</code>, which have not been implemented yet, I believe.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>bigOpS</code> has been implemented, see <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/blob/424b711c56aaedd0e0c2e27d26ed2283a4702274/src/Iris/Algebra/BigOp.lean#L482\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/blob/424b711c56aaedd0e0c2e27d26ed2283a4702274/src/Iris/Algebra/BigOp.lean#L482</a></p>",
        "id": 585186633,
        "sender_full_name": "Zongyuan Liu",
        "timestamp": 1776086078
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hmm, how did I miss that</p>",
        "id": 585186839,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1776086139
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ok, I gave it another look. The reason I missed it was that there was no <code>[∗set]</code> notation for <code>bigOpS sep</code> and similar.</p>",
        "id": 585376352,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1776159655
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Also, it seems like the <code>bigOpS_commute</code> equivalent lemma for <code>bigOpS</code> doesn't really follow the same format as the other <code>_commute</code> lemmas for lists and maps. I like best the <code>BigOpS.hom</code> convention over <code>BigOpL.bigOpL_hom</code>, but <code>BigOpS</code> receives its <code>MonoidHomomorphism</code> instance as a regular hypothesis, not a typeclass one. </p>\n<div class=\"spoiler-block\"><div class=\"spoiler-header\">\n<p>BigOpS.hom</p>\n</div><div class=\"spoiler-content\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/blob/424b711c56aaedd0e0c2e27d26ed2283a4702274/src/Iris/Algebra/BigOp.lean#L571-L577\">See in Github</a></p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">S'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LawfulFiniteSet</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M₂</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MonoidHomomorphism</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unit₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unit₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M₁</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">([</span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">set</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">([</span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">set</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"spoiler-block\"><div class=\"spoiler-header\">\n<p>BigOpL.bigOpL_hom</p>\n</div><div class=\"spoiler-content\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/blob/424b711c56aaedd0e0c2e27d26ed2283a4702274/src/Iris/Algebra/BigOp.lean#L234-L239\">See in Github</a></p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bigOpL_hom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MonoidHomomorphism</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unit₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unit₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M₁</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">([</span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">list</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">([</span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">list</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n</div></div>\n<p>Any reasons to prefer one version over the other?</p>",
        "id": 585377570,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1776160003
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"690100\">Fernando LEAL</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/490604-iris-lean/topic/Adding.20missing.20definitions.20to.20.60Plainly.2Elean.60/near/585377570\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Also, it seems like the <code>bigOpS_commute</code> equivalent lemma for <code>bigOpS</code> doesn't really follow the same format as the other <code>_commute</code> lemmas for lists and maps. I like best the <code>BigOpS.hom</code> convention over <code>BigOpL.bigOpL_hom</code>, but <code>BigOpS</code> receives its <code>MonoidHomomorphism</code> instance as a regular hypothesis, not a typeclass one. </p>\n<div class=\"spoiler-block\"><div class=\"spoiler-header\">\n<p>BigOpS.hom</p>\n</div><div class=\"spoiler-content\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/blob/424b711c56aaedd0e0c2e27d26ed2283a4702274/src/Iris/Algebra/BigOp.lean#L571-L577\">See in Github</a></p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">S'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LawfulFiniteSet</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M₂</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MonoidHomomorphism</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unit₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unit₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M₁</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">S'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">([</span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">set</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">([</span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">set</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"spoiler-block\"><div class=\"spoiler-header\">\n<p>BigOpL.bigOpL_hom</p>\n</div><div class=\"spoiler-content\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/blob/424b711c56aaedd0e0c2e27d26ed2283a4702274/src/Iris/Algebra/BigOp.lean#L234-L239\">See in Github</a></p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bigOpL_hom</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MonoidHomomorphism</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unit₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unit₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M₁</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">([</span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">list</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">([</span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">op₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">list</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n</div></div>\n<p>Any reasons to prefer one version over the other?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I think it is simply because the lemmas were done by two different people. You are welcome to change them to what you think is easier to work with (and make the names consistent: <code>BigOpS.hom</code> -&gt; <code>BigOpS.bigOpS_hom</code>).</p>",
        "id": 585451645,
        "sender_full_name": "Zongyuan Liu",
        "timestamp": 1776178990
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I actually like <code>BigOpS.hom</code> better, it does away with a needless repetation of <code>bigOpS</code>. This PR was recently closed though, so I guess I'll open a new one. However, I would also push to remove the <code>bigOpS_</code> prefix from all lemmas in <a href=\"https://github.com/lean-community/iris-lean/blob/fele/feat/add-plainly-definitions/src/Iris/Algebra/BigOp.lean#L576\">that file</a>, nost just those of <code>hom</code>.</p>",
        "id": 585452923,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1776179201
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"690100\">Fernando LEAL</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/490604-iris-lean/topic/Adding.20missing.20definitions.20to.20.60Plainly.2Elean.60/near/585164999\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>By the way, I wanted to ask about the <code>PartialMap</code> API, and what are our plans on it. I found it a bit painful to use since many times the type arguments such as <code>M</code> and <code>K</code> need to be given explicitly, although I'm not sure how we could fix it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>As a follow up on this, how bad would it be to just have <code>K</code> live inside of the <code>PartialMap</code> instance, as a field? I sketched out how that would look like in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/pull/312\">the following PR</a>. It's a bit more cumbersome to write the type of the key, but as far as I've seen it removes all prior problems of types not being inferred correctly (which were my main complaints).</p>\n<p>Actually, the issue with this representation is when trying to talk about two maps which share the same <code>K</code> (such as <code>bigSepM_impl_strong</code>)</p>",
        "id": 585523260,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1776200926
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, that is one issue with bundling.</p>",
        "id": 585635071,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1776252636
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>As an alternative, we might consider changing K to an outParam.</p>",
        "id": 585635336,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1776252723
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Isn't it already an outParam?</p>",
        "id": 585635395,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1776252740
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ah, it is</p>",
        "id": 585635696,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1776252816
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe not of <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/blob/master/src%2FIris%2FStd%2FPartialMap.lean#L50-L52\"><code>FiniteMap</code></a>? I guess lean doesn't propagate the <code>outParam</code> of <code>K</code> when extending <code>PartialMap</code>.</p>",
        "id": 585635755,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1776252834
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Not sure if this is the case, but that'd be subtle</p>",
        "id": 585636269,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1776253004
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>As a general comment: it's great to note pains when porting stuff, but I think changes like this should be justified primarily by the pains of people using the library. For example I suspect that maps will mostly be specialized (unlike the library, which is intentionally general) meaning these annotations will be less common. </p>\n<p>Bundling the key type may or may not be a good idea, but we can only really evaluate it after it's been applied to some real examples!</p>",
        "id": 585638822,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1776253819
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I will mention that I am also working on porting a logic, including some resources that use <a href=\"https://github.com/markusdemedeiros/Metrology/blob/main/Metrology/Iris/SpecProgram.lean\">maps</a>, so we can get more data for this soon :)</p>",
        "id": 585639112,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1776253913
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I agree with the idea that when making changes, the priority are users of Iris, not developers of Iris. However, I don't think this instance is one such problem. I think the issue was that we forgot to make <code>K</code> an <code>outParam</code> in the <code>FiniteMap</code> and <code>LawfulFiniteMap</code> definitions. See <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/pull/313\">this PR</a> which allows us to remove may of the <code>(K := K)</code> that we had in the code.</p>",
        "id": 585641044,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1776254480
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>These are the relevant changes, and what I meant by \"I guess Lean doesn't propagate the <code>outParam</code> of <code>K</code> when extending <code>PartialMap</code>.<br>\n<img alt=\"image.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1351x682\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/3121/oqRCyWBSzYxih_kVi4sOcZJZ/image.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/3121/oqRCyWBSzYxih_kVi4sOcZJZ/image.png/840x560.webp\"></p>",
        "id": 585641388,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1776254549
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The idea of including the <code>K</code> parameter inside the definition was just something I tried because I thought <code>K</code>was already an <code>outParam</code>. I had mistakenly seen the definition of <code>PartialMap</code> and assumed it was the same for <code>FiniteMap</code>. My bad if such a big PR ruffled a few feathers <span aria-label=\"pray\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f64f\" role=\"img\" title=\"pray\">:pray:</span>, it was never meant to be more than a draft</p>",
        "id": 585641799,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1776254654
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>No no! That's a great idea, and I also wrongly assumed the same thing :) I was commenting on the idea of bundling the key type.</p>",
        "id": 585643322,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1776255050
    }
]